Streamer: the queuedFrameNum comment describes the ring the Streamer allocates.

NRIStreamer.h says StreamerDesc::queuedFrameNum "adds 1 under the hood for the current
"not-yet-committed" frame". It did after 3f38b1a ("clarified that ring buffers are allocated for
queuedFrameNum + 1 frames"), until 6f80a7c ("fixed wrong ring-buffer logic") made the dynamic
buffer a ring of per-frame regions and dropped m_Desc.queuedFrameNum++, leaving the comment. Since
then StreamerImpl::Grow sizes the dynamic buffer as m_DynamicBufferSizePerFrame * queuedFrameNum,
EndFrame advances m_FrameIndex modulo queuedFrameNum, and a dynamic buffer replaced by Grow is kept
for queuedFrameNum more frames. That is queuedFrameNum regions, the current frame's among them. A
caller who believes the comment and passes the number of frames the GPU may still be executing
while it fills the next one gets one region too few: the frame being filled overwrites the region
the oldest of them may still be reading.

This changes the comment to what the code does: queuedFrameNum counts the current frame, so data
streamed in a frame is overwritten queuedFrameNum frames later, and the usual values become 2-3.
The code is unchanged. Making it add 1 again would also fit the old comment, but would give every
caller that sized for the current behaviour one more region of memory. Measured on Vulkan and
D3D12: with queuedFrameNum = 2, data streamed in frames 0 and 1 lands in two regions, and frame 2
reuses frame 0's offset.

Applies on top of NRI v190, independent of the other patches.

diff --git a/Include/Extensions/NRIStreamer.h b/Include/Extensions/NRIStreamer.h
index a6ff35f..86fe2f8 100644
--- a/Include/Extensions/NRIStreamer.h
+++ b/Include/Extensions/NRIStreamer.h
@@ -33,7 +33,7 @@ NriStruct(StreamerDesc) {
     // Dynamically (re)allocated ring-buffer for copying and rendering
     Nri(MemoryLocation) dynamicBufferMemoryLocation;                // UPLOAD or DEVICE_UPLOAD
     Nri(BufferDesc) dynamicBufferDesc;                              // "size" is ignored
-    uint32_t queuedFrameNum;                                        // number of frames "in-flight" (usually 1-3), adds 1 under the hood for the current "not-yet-committed" frame
+    uint32_t queuedFrameNum;                                        // number of frames "in-flight" (usually 2-3), including the current "not-yet-committed" frame, i.e. data streamed in a frame is overwritten "queuedFrameNum" frames later
 
     // Statically allocated read/write ring-buffer for generic CPU data
     NriOptional uint64_t hostDataCapacity;                          // capacity for all simultaneously live data and alignment padding
